{"id": "28B-65", "cog": 3, "sci": "ok", "key": "ok", "scope": "in", "topic": "ok", "d": "ok", "distractors": "ok", "feedback": "good", "hint": "good", "figure": "ok", "dup": "28B-44", "calc": "na", "action": "REPLACE", "defects": ["exact duplicate of 28B-44: same reaction/question and same four choices (only stem wording/topic tag differ); both could appear in one exam"], "rewrite": {"t": "alcohols", "d": 2, "k": 0, "q": "Ethyl 4-oxocyclohexane-1-carboxylate is treated with NaBH<sub>4</sub> in CH<sub>3</sub>OH at 0 °C, then with water. What is the major organic product?", "c": ["ethyl 4-hydroxycyclohexane-1-carboxylate", "4-(hydroxymethyl)cyclohexan-1-one", "4-(hydroxymethyl)cyclohexan-1-ol", "4-oxocyclohexane-1-carboxylic acid"], "h": "NaBH<sub>4</sub> is a mild hydride donor. Which carbonyl is more electrophilic, the ring ketone or the ester?", "w": "NaBH<sub>4</sub> reduces ketones quickly but esters only very slowly, because the ester C=O is stabilized by donation from its OR oxygen. Only the ring C=O becomes CH–OH and the ester survives: <b>ethyl 4-hydroxycyclohexane-1-carboxylate</b> (a cis/trans mixture). Reducing the ester instead requires protecting the ketone and using LiAlH<sub>4</sub>.", "x": ["", "This reverses the selectivity: the ester is the less reactive carbonyl, so NaBH<sub>4</sub> cannot reduce it while leaving the ketone.", "Reducing both carbonyls is the LiAlH<sub>4</sub> result; NaBH<sub>4</sub> does not reduce the ester at a useful rate.", "NaBH<sub>4</sub> is a reducing agent; it does not hydrolyze the ester, and the ketone would not survive it."]}}
{"id": "28B-66", "cog": 1, "sci": "ok", "key": "ok", "scope": "in", "topic": "ok", "d": "ok", "distractors": "strong", "feedback": "good", "hint": "good", "figure": "na", "dup": "", "calc": "na", "action": "KEEP", "defects": []}
{"id": "28B-67", "cog": 3, "sci": "ok", "key": "ok", "scope": "in", "topic": "ok", "d": "ok", "distractors": "strong", "feedback": "good", "hint": "good", "figure": "ok", "dup": "", "calc": "RDKit: ketone C3 = R; carbinol branch keeps rank 1 so C3 stays R in both products; verified", "action": "KEEP", "defects": []}
{"id": "28B-68", "cog": 3, "sci": "ok", "key": "ok", "scope": "in", "topic": "ok", "d": "ok", "distractors": "ok", "feedback": "good", "hint": "good", "figure": "ok", "dup": "28B-33", "calc": "na", "action": "REPLACE", "defects": ["exact duplicate of 28B-33: same reaction/question and same four choices (only stem wording/topic tag differ); both could appear in one exam"], "rewrite": {"t": "alcohols", "d": 3, "k": 0, "q": "δ-Valerolactone (oxan-2-one, a six-membered lactone) is treated with 1 equivalent of DIBAL-H in toluene at −78 °C, then with water. What is the main product isolated?", "c": ["oxan-2-ol (a cyclic hemiacetal)", "pentane-1,5-diol (an open diol)", "oxane (a cyclic ether)", "5-hydroxypentanoic acid"], "h": "At −78 °C DIBAL-H delivers one hydride to an ester, and the intermediate collapses only at workup. What does that give when the ester is a ring?", "w": "One hydride adds to the lactone C=O; at −78 °C the aluminum-bound tetrahedral intermediate does not collapse. Workup releases 5-hydroxypentanal, whose OH adds to its own aldehyde to form the favored six-membered cyclic hemiacetal: <b>oxan-2-ol (a lactol)</b>. Excess LiAlH<sub>4</sub> would instead give pentane-1,5-diol.", "x": ["", "The diol needs a second hydride, as with LiAlH<sub>4</sub>; one equivalent of DIBAL-H at −78 °C stops at the aldehyde oxidation level.", "Hydride reagents do not remove the carbonyl oxygen completely to leave a cyclic ether.", "DIBAL-H is a reducing agent; opening the ring to the hydroxy acid would need aqueous base or acid, not hydride."]}}
{"id": "28B-69", "cog": 3, "sci": "ok", "key": "ok", "scope": "in", "topic": "ok", "d": "ok", "distractors": "strong", "feedback": "good", "hint": "good", "figure": "ok", "dup": "", "calc": "na", "action": "KEEP", "defects": []}
